\description{
Transcribes a recording and writes its pauses and speakers back into the
text, so \code{measure(text = )} reads a transcript that carries them. A plain
transcript records what was said and discards how. Pauses, hesitation and who
was speaking leave with it, and for a construct that lives in delivery rather
than in wording that is the absence of the thing being measured.
}
\section{Caching}{

A transcript already on disk is read instead of transcribing again, so an
interrupted run resumes and a hand-corrected transcript survives. \code{force = TRUE} returns to the recognizer.

What counts as already done is the question \code{\link[=measure]{measure()}} asks of its
checkpoints, which is whether the work was done under the settings now being
requested rather than merely done. \code{tool}, \code{asr_model}, \code{diarize_names},
\code{language} and \code{extra} are recorded in a \code{.fathom.json} beside each
transcript, and a run whose settings differ transcribes again. Without that,
a call with \code{diarize_names} off in a directory that already holds a diarized
transcript would read the diarized one and quietly ignore the argument.

\code{annotate_timing}, \code{min_pause} and \code{speakers} are not in that record, since
none of them reaches the recognizer. Changing a rendering costs nothing and
re-reads the same transcript.

A transcript with no record beside it is used rather than discarded, because
that is what a hand-corrected one looks like. Its \code{tool}, \code{asr_model} and
\code{extra} come back \code{NA}, since naming settings that did not produce those
words would be worse than saying nothing.
}

\section{Transcript format}{

The row a model is asked about is the whole recording, and the structure
inside it is written into \code{text} rather than spread across rows.

Inside a row, a line is a turn, meaning a maximal run of words by one
speaker. Nothing splits a turn further. A silence inside one person's talk is
written where it happened, as \verb{[pause 0.4s]}, which says how long it was
rather than only that it passed a threshold.

Silence at a turn boundary is written \verb{[gap 0.8s]} and belongs to neither
speaker, so it goes on a line of its own. Whether a silence is a hesitation
or a turn transition is a fact about the recording, so the two markers are
told apart by whether the timings carry a speaker, not by whether labels were
asked for.

Lines are newline separated with the speaker in brackets ahead of them.
}

\section{Speakers}{

Speakers reach the output only from the recognizer. Absent, \code{n_speakers} and
\code{n_turns} are \code{NA} and \code{diarized} is \code{FALSE}, so a transcript with no speaker
in it is never read as a transcript with one speaker in it.

\code{speakers} renames what the recognizer emitted, since \code{SPEAKER_00} carries
nothing a model can use. It is also the fastest way to put the answer in the
prompt. A speaker renamed \verb{Angry Protester} has been coded before the model
saw the text, and nothing in the results would show it.
}

\section{Pause and gap counts}{

\code{n_pauses} and \code{n_gaps} count what \code{min_pause} found in the timings, so they
hold with \code{annotate_timing} off, where the text shows nothing. Only \code{text}
and \code{annotate_timing} change with the rendering.

None of them reaches the model, since \code{measure()} reads \code{id} and \code{text} and
nothing else.

\code{n_pauses} also moves with diarization on the same words, because without
turns a turn transition is just another silence.
}

